{# ── Chapter: Hook ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="hook">
  <h2>The dashboard refreshed and every project the user had open was just gone</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      An app loads a list of projects into state on mount, one <code>fetch</code>, stashed in a <code>useState</code> or a store, and renders it. It works fine on office wifi. Then someone accidentally hits refresh, or their laptop wakes from sleep and re-runs the page, and the list is empty again, replaced by a loading spinner. If the network happens to be down at that exact moment, tunnel, elevator, flaky coffee-shop wifi, the spinner never resolves into anything. The data the user was just looking at ten seconds ago is simply gone, and the app has no way to get it back except asking the network again.
    </p>
    <p>
      Nothing was ever actually saved. It was held in memory for as long as the page happened to stay open, which is not the same thing as being cached.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      In-memory state, a <code>useState</code>, a Redux store, a plain module-level variable, only exists for the lifetime of the current JS runtime. A reload doesn't "reset" that state; it destroys the JS context entirely and starts a new one with nothing in it. The app's only path back to real data is the network, and if that request fails, there's no fallback layer underneath it. Teams frequently call this "caching" because it avoids a re-render or a redundant fetch mid-session, but it provides zero resilience to the two things that actually break user sessions: reloads and connectivity gaps.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The real gap is between three things that get casually conflated: keeping a value in memory to avoid re-computing it, persisting a value across page loads, and supporting genuine offline use. A JS variable does only the first. <code>localStorage</code> can do the second for small string data, synchronously, but blocks the main thread on large payloads and caps out around 5&ndash;10MB per origin. Neither one is a client-side database. IndexedDB is the browser's actual transactional, structured-storage engine, per-origin, disk-backed, capable of storing real JS objects (not just strings), and it's the only one of the three that supports the read-model pattern this case is about: paint instantly from what's on disk, then revalidate over the network.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Concept ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="concept">
  <h2>IndexedDB gives you a real client-side database, at the cost of an async, transaction-based API</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      IndexedDB stores structured JavaScript objects (not just strings, like <code>localStorage</code>) in named "object stores" inside a per-origin database. Every read and write happens inside a transaction, and every operation is asynchronous, you get a request object back and listen for a <code>success</code> or <code>error</code> event (or use a promise wrapper), rather than getting a value back immediately the way <code>localStorage.getItem()</code> does. In exchange for that extra step, whatever gets written is genuinely persisted: it survives a reload, closing the tab, even restarting the browser, because it's written to disk, not held in memory.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The basic pattern is <code>indexedDB.open(name, version)</code>, which fires <code>onupgradeneeded</code> the first time (or on a version bump) to create object stores and indexes, then <code>onsuccess</code> with a usable <code>db</code> handle. Reads and writes go through <code>db.transaction([store], mode).objectStore(store).put(obj)</code> or <code>.getAll()</code>, each returning a request you attach <code>onsuccess</code>/<code>onerror</code> to. That's meaningfully more ceremony than <code>localStorage.setItem(key, JSON.stringify(value))</code>, but it buys real structured storage: whole objects and arrays without manual serialization, indexed queries, and a quota that's typically an order of magnitude larger than <code>localStorage</code>'s 5&ndash;10MB (often hundreds of MB to several GB, scaled to available disk).
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The sharpest footgun in raw IndexedDB is transaction lifetime: a transaction auto-commits as soon as it has no pending IDB requests queued in the current turn of the event loop. If you <code>await</code> something unrelated, a <code>fetch()</code>, a <code>setTimeout</code>, in the middle of a transaction and then try to issue another request against it, the transaction has often already closed, and you get a <code>TransactionInactiveError</code>. This is why idiomatic IndexedDB code looks like a chain of synchronous-looking request calls inside one transaction scope, and it's exactly the awkwardness promise-based wrapper libraries like <code>idb</code> exist to smooth over, without changing the underlying constraint: keep async work outside the transaction, keep the transaction itself tight.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Story ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="story-1">
  <h2>The team that cached in `useState` until the train wifi cut out</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A small team building a project tracker kept the project list in a top-level <code>useState</code>, populated by one <code>fetch</code> on mount. It shipped, worked in every demo, and nobody thought about it again until a user on a commuter train reported that the app "loses everything" whenever the connection drops. Losing signal mid-session didn't just pause the app, it meant the next reload (or the phone's browser silently re-creating the tab) came back to a blank list and a spinner that had nothing to resolve to, because the fetch it depended on had nothing to talk to.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Once support started tagging the pattern, it showed up constantly: commutes, coffee shops, spotty office wifi. Any reload during a network gap produced the same failure, a totally blank state, not even the item the user had open moments before, because nothing before the in-memory <code>useState</code> had ever actually been saved anywhere.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The fix the team reached for first, and rejected, was a full offline-first service worker layer: intercept every request, cache every asset, sync in the background. That's a real pattern, but it's a much bigger project than the actual problem warranted. The narrower fix: after every successful <code>fetch</code>, write the normalized response into an IndexedDB object store as the app's read model. On load, read from IndexedDB first, near-instant despite being async, to paint something on screen immediately, then kick off the real network fetch in the background to revalidate and overwrite both the UI and the IndexedDB store once it resolves. When the network fails, the IndexedDB-sourced data just stays visible. Stale beats blank.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

