{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>IndexedDB isn't free, and not every cache needs it</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      IndexedDB is more code than a plain in-memory variable, opening a database, defining object stores, writing async handlers. That cost is worth paying when losing the data on reload or offline genuinely hurts the user, a project list someone was mid-edit on, a form draft, anything slow or annoying to refetch. If the data is cheap and fast to refetch and staleness for a second or two doesn't matter, plain memory state (or even nothing at all, just refetch on every mount) is simpler and has less to maintain, and adding IndexedDB there is complexity with no real payoff.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      A library like <code>idb</code> (a small promise-based wrapper) removes most of the callback ceremony without changing the underlying transaction rules, worth reaching for the moment a project has more than one or two IndexedDB call sites. Schema changes go through the same <code>onupgradeneeded</code> hook, keyed to a version number, so plan the object store's shape with some headroom; migrating existing IndexedDB data on a schema change is real, if straightforward, work.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      This pattern, read from cache first, then revalidate over the network, is the same "stale-while-revalidate" idea that HTTP caching and libraries like SWR/React Query apply to network responses; the difference here is that the cache layer is a real disk-backed store instead of an in-memory query cache, so it survives a reload and works fully offline, not just across component remounts within one session. It composes with a service worker for full offline asset support, but doesn't require one, this pattern alone gets an app most of the perceived resilience of "offline mode" for its data layer, without taking on request interception or asset caching at all.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

